package cajeta.nucleo.transform;

/**
 * The transform-intrinsic surface (transform-intrinsics spec §2-§6): the
 * trusted, value-level combinators `Grad` / `Jit` / `Vmap` / `Pmap`, and the
 * annotation sugar `@Grad` / `@Jit` / `@Vmap` that desugars onto them.
 *
 * These are COMPILER INTRINSICS, not methods of this class: a bare call
 * `Grad(f)` on a statically-known function is recognized and transformed at
 * compile time, and its type is transform-polymorphic — each combinator maps a
 * function TYPE to a different function type, which is beyond what a single
 * declared generic signature can express. This file is the importable home of
 * the contract; the types a caller actually names live beside it
 * ([[GradResult]]).
 *
 * The combinators, nearest-the-declaration-first under composition (spec §4;
 * `@Jit @Vmap @Grad f` == `Jit(Vmap(Grad(f)))`):
 *
 * - `Grad(f)`: for `f : (P...) -> S` with scalar-valued `S`, gives
 *   `(P...) -> GradResult<S, G>` — the forward value and the gradient w.r.t.
 *   argument 0 (`Grad<N>(f)` selects argument N). Scalar and Tensor arguments;
 *   second-order via `Grad(Grad(f))` (scalar). `@NoGrad` on a called helper
 *   makes it a constant leaf.
 * - `Vmap(f)`: for `f : (T) -> R`, gives `(T[]) -> #R[]` — `f` lifted over a
 *   leading batch axis with no hand-written loop. `Vmap(Grad(f))` batches the
 *   differentiated form (per-example gradients).
 * - `Jit(f)`: same signature as `f`, body fused (temporaries eliminated).
 *   Composes over the others: `Jit(Vmap(Grad(f)))` fuses the batched,
 *   differentiated form.
 * - `Pmap(f)`: reserved (spec F8) — recognized, reports
 *   CAJETA_ERROR_TRANSFORM_PMAP_UNIMPLEMENTED in v1.
 *
 * Every misuse is a named, located compile error: a runtime-only function
 * value is CAJETA_ERROR_TRANSFORM_NOT_SPECIALIZABLE, an op outside the
 * differentiable/batchable set names the operator, and a rank mismatch names
 * the ranks and dtype.
 */
public final class Transforms {
    private Transforms() { }
}
